 "w": 14,
 "title": "Question words: qui, que, où, quand, comment",
 "goal": "By the end of this lesson you can ask who, what, where, when, how, how much and why in French, in both standard and casual word orders, and understand Quebec questions like C'est quoi? and Comment ça coûte?",
 "learn": [
  {
   "h": "The question words",
   "body": [
    "These words open information questions (questions that can't be answered with yes or no). Learn them as a set.",
    "*que* becomes *qu'* before a vowel. *Quel* (which, what) agrees with its noun: *quel*, *quelle*, *quels*, *quelles*, all pronounced the same."
   ],
   "table": {
    "head": [
     "Word",
     "Meaning",
     "Example"
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "qui",
      "who",
      "Qui est là?"
     ],
     [
      "que / qu'est-ce que",
      "what",
      "Qu'est-ce que tu fais?"
     ],
     [
      "où",
      "where",
      "Où est la gare?"
     ],
     [
      "quand",
      "when",
      "Quand est-ce que tu arrives?"
     ],
     [
      "comment",
      "how",
      "Comment ça va?"
     ],
     [
      "combien",
      "how much, how many",
      "Combien ça coûte?"
     ],
     [
      "pourquoi",
      "why",
      "Pourquoi tu ris?"
     ],
     [
      "quel / quelle",
      "which, what",
      "Quelle heure est-il?"
     ]
    ]
   },
   "tip": "Pourquoi? — Parce que… (Why? — Because…)"
  },
  {
   "h": "Three word orders",
   "body": [
    "Just like yes/no questions, information questions come in three registers. Casual: question word at the END (*Tu vas où?*) or at the start with normal order (*Où tu vas?*). Neutral: question word + *est-ce que* (*Où est-ce que tu vas?*). Formal: inversion (*Où vas-tu?*).",
    "All three are correct French; choose by situation."
   ],
   "table": {
    "head": [
     "Casual",
     "Neutral",
     "Formal"
    ],
    "rows": [
     [
      "Tu vas où?",
      "Où est-ce que tu vas?",
      "Où vas-tu?"
     ],
     [
      "Tu arrives quand?",
      "Quand est-ce que tu arrives?",
      "Quand arrives-tu?"
     ],
     [
      "Tu fais quoi?",
      "Qu'est-ce que tu fais?",
      "Que fais-tu?"
     ],
     [
      "Vous êtes combien?",
      "Combien est-ce que vous êtes?",
      "Combien êtes-vous?"
     ]
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "h": "'What': que, qu'est-ce que, quoi",
   "body": [
    "'What' has three forms depending on position. At the start: *Qu'est-ce que tu veux?* (neutral) or *Que veux-tu?* (formal). At the end or after a preposition: *quoi*. *Tu veux quoi?* *Avec quoi?* *C'est quoi?*",
    "*Qu'est-ce que c'est?* (What is it?) is the standard everyday form; *C'est quoi?* is its casual twin, very frequent in Quebec."
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Qui and quel",
   "body": [
    "*Qui* (who) can be the subject directly: *Qui parle?* *Qui veut un café?* It can also follow a preposition: *Avec qui?* *C'est à qui?* (Whose is it?)",
    "*Quel* asks 'which' or 'what' before a noun: *Quel jour?* *Quelle heure?* With *être*: *Quel est ton nom? Quelle est ton adresse?*"
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Quebec: c'est quoi, comment ça coûte",
   "body": [
    "Quebecers love *c'est quoi* (what is): *C'est quoi, ton nom?* *C'est quoi le problème?* Casual speech also shortens *qu'est-ce que* to *qu'est-ce*: *Qu'est-ce tu fais?* (sounds like 'kess tu fais').",
    "For prices, you will often hear *Comment ça coûte?* or *Ça coûte combien?* instead of the standard *Combien ça coûte?* Also: *Où c'est que tu vas?* (where are you going?) and *Comment ça s'appelle?*. These are informal; use *combien* in writing."
   ]
  }
 ],
